# TAR(1)

## NAME

tar - archiver

## SYNOPSIS

| **tar** key **f** archive [ **b** factor ] [ name ... ]

## DESCRIPTION

*Tar* saves and restores files. Its actions are controlled by the *key* argument. The *key* is
a string of characters containing at most one function letter and possibly one or more function
modifiers. Other arguments to the command are file or directory names specifying which files
are to be dumped or restored. In all cases, appearance of a directory name refers to the files
and (recursively) subdirectories of that directory.

**Note:** v7's *tar* wrote on magnetic tape and defaulted to **/dev/mt1**. This machine has no
tape drive and no driver for one, so the **f** modifier is **mandatory**: an archive is a file,
a pipe, or a raw disk device. The drive-selecting digits **0** through **7** are gone with the
default, and so is the **u** key; see NOTES.

The function portion of the key is specified by one of the following letters:

**r**
: The named files are written on the end of the archive. The **c** function implies this.

**x**
: The named files are extracted from the archive. If the named file matches a directory whose
  contents had been written onto the archive, this directory is (recursively) extracted. The
  owner, modification time, and mode are restored (if possible). If no file argument is given,
  the entire content of the archive is extracted. Note that if multiple entries specifying the
  same file are on the archive, the last one overwrites all earlier.

**t**
: The names of the specified files are listed each time they occur on the archive. If no file
  argument is given, all of the names on the archive are listed.

**c**
: Create a new archive; writing begins at the beginning of the archive instead of after the last
  file. This command implies **r.**

The following characters may be used in addition to the letter which selects the function
desired.

**v**
: Normally *tar* does its work silently. The **v** (verbose) option causes it to type the name of
  each file it treats preceded by the function letter. With the **t** function, **v** gives more
  information about the archive entries than just the name.

**w**
: causes *tar* to print the action to be taken followed by file name, then wait for user
  confirmation. If a word beginning with `y` is given, the action is performed. Any other input,
  and end of file, means don't do it.

**f**
: causes *tar* to use the next argument as the name of the archive. It is not optional. If the
  name of the file is `-`, tar writes to standard output or reads from standard input, whichever
  is appropriate. Thus, *tar* can be used as the head or tail of a filter chain *Tar* can also be
  used to move hierarchies with the command cd fromdir; tar cf - . | (cd todir; tar xf -)

**b**
: causes *tar* to use the next argument as the blocking factor for records. The default is 1 for
  a file or a pipe and 6 for a raw device; the maximum is 20. Reading needs no blocking factor at
  all and ignores this one: records come out of the buffer in the order they went in however they
  were grouped. See NOTES for the rule a raw device imposes.

**l**
: tells *tar* to complain if it cannot resolve all of the links to the files dumped. If this is
  not specified, no error messages are printed.

**m**
: tells *tar* to not restore the modification times. The mod time will be the time of extraction.

## NOTES

### A NAME IS 100 BYTES OUT AND 18 BACK

The archive format gives a name 100 bytes, and a name of exactly 100 carries no terminator. A
directory entry on this filesystem holds **18** (**DIRSIZ**, see types(5)), so a path with long
components is archived faithfully and **cannot be restored under the same name:** each
component is truncated to 18 characters as it is created. Names survive the round trip in one
direction only. A path longer than 100 bytes is refused outright, with a diagnostic, and so is
a directory whose own path leaves no room for the names under it.

### AN ARCHIVE ON A RAW DEVICE

*Tar* can write straight onto a raw drive with no filesystem on it -- **/dev/rmd4**, say,
which is unassigned -- and that is how a tree leaves this machine. Do not name **/dev/rmd1**:
that is the mounted */usr*. A raw transfer goes through *physio*(), which requires the count to be a
whole number of 3072-byte filesystem blocks and the buffer to lie on a 512-word boundary. 512
times the blocking factor is a multiple of 3072 only when the factor is a multiple of **6**, so
on a character special the default is 6 and a factor that is not a multiple of 6 is refused
with a diagnostic.

**r** is refused on a raw device. Appending means stepping back over the archive's terminating
record, and a seek of one 512-byte record is not a whole number of blocks; *physio*() truncates
such an offset silently and would position the write on the wrong block.

Nothing stops *tar* being pointed at **/dev/rmd0**, which is the pack the system is running on.
It is mode 0600 and root's for that reason.

### WHAT WAS REMOVED

The **u** key is not implemented. It kept an index of names and modification times in **/tmp**
and sorted and de-duplicated it by running sort(1) and awk(1) through sh(1); there is no *awk*
on this system. Use **c** to rebuild an archive or **r** to add to one.

The **0** through **7** modifiers, which selected a tape drive, are not implemented.

### BLOCKS HERE ARE 512 BYTES

An archive record is 512 bytes and a physical block is 512 times the blocking factor. These are
the *archive's* units and not this filesystem's, whose block is 3072 bytes: the numbers are the
format's, they are the same on every machine that has ever written a tar archive, and changing
them would make an archive written here readable nowhere else. *Tar* is the exception to the
rule that a block reported to a user is 1024 bytes (*df*(1M)).

## FILES

/bin/mkdir

## DIAGNOSTICS

| Complaints about bad key characters and archive read/write errors.
| Complaints if enough memory is not available to hold the link tables.

## BUGS

| There is no way to ask for the *n*-th occurrence of a file.
| Device errors are handled ungracefully.
| The **b** option should not be used with archives that are going to be updated.
| A name is stored exactly as it was given on the command line, so **tar cf arch /a/b** stores **/a/b/...** and extracting it later writes over the original wherever the extraction is done from. Name the tree relatively - the **f** entry's **cd fromdir** idiom - unless that is what is wanted.
| No record is written for a directory itself, only for the files under it, so a directory that is empty is not archived and the modes of the directories an extraction creates are mkdir(1)'s and not the original's.
| The current limit on file name length is 100 characters, and a restored name is limited to 18 per component; see NOTES.
| A file that cannot be opened, or whose name is too long, is reported and skipped, and the exit status is still zero.
| The walk is limited to 12 levels of directory nesting: it holds one open descriptor per level, and **NOFILE** is 20.
